(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609113000-the-language-level-findings-were-two-causes-not-thirteen-defects")
  (:adr/topic "q9-language-level-findings")
  (:adr/title "The thirteen language-level findings were two causes: one compiler gap, and a library with no type")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema"
                      "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang" "kotoba-lang/amu"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110500-the-q9-procedures-other-half-a-doctor-that-fixes-forward"
                        "adr-2609101900-q9-rename-reverted-pending-admission"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

Owner instruction 2026-09-11: fix the language-level defects.

`kotoba doctor` had reduced the Q9 wave-1 corpus -- twenty reverted
repositories, 29 files renamed to .kotoba -- from one repeated admission
message to thirteen `:kotoba.error/*` findings. Those thirteen were the
instruction's subject.

## They were not thirteen defects

Counted by cause rather than by file: **eight distinct defects in nine files**,
and four of the thirteen findings were ONE declaration. `datom.source` is
required by datalog.index, datalog.query and datom.source.adjacency, so its
single refusal was reported four times -- amu names the ENTRY it was asked
about, not the module that failed.

Then the eight split cleanly in two, and the split is the whole finding:

| cause | findings | disposition |
|---|---|---|
| a protocol may not carry documentation | 4 | compiler gap -- **fixed** |
| kotoba.set has no type | 9 | **blocked**, recorded |

## The compiler gap

`ns` and `defn` both admit a docstring under `max-*-docstring-chars`.
`defprotocol` and `definterface` did not: a leading string sat in the first
METHOD position and a trailing one after a parameter vector, and both were
measured against a shape wanting exactly `(name [params])`.

    (defprotocol IP (-scan [this pattern]))                 exit 0
    (defprotocol IP \"the one operation\" (-scan [this p]))   exit 65
    (defprotocol IP (-scan [this pattern] \"method doc\"))     exit 65

both refused with `defprotocol requires unique bounded (method [this ...])
signatures` -- a sentence about signatures, raised by a string that is not
one, which sends the reader to the parameter vectors.

This was worth fixing rather than working around because the only way past it
was to DELETE the text. `IPatternSource`'s docstring states the three
invariants every implementation must satisfy. A migration that requires
deleting the contract in order to compile is narrowing the component, which is
exactly what ADR-q9 forbids. Nothing in the bounded closed-world dispatch
profile depended on a docstring's absence: documentation does not reach
dispatch.

Landed: kotoba-sema 8b288047, both docstrings bounded by the same limit
`defn` applies. Re-measured through amu at the advanced pin, all four modules
move past that class.

## The library with no type

The other nine are `kotoba.set` and its eleven sibling repositories, and they
are `:blocked`. **There is no type variable anywhere in this surface.** A
parameter is annotated with a concrete value type or it defaults to `:i64`:

    (defn f [s] (count s))
      refused -- count requires a bounded vector, a typed set or a canonical
      typed map; got :i64

    (defn f [s1 [:set :i64] s2 [:set :i64]] :bool (<= (count s1) (count s2)))
      admitted

So the blocker is the TYPE and not the shape of the code -- and supplying the
type is exactly what narrows. Measured on the callers: the same functions
carry three element types inside ONE program. `datalog.core` uses
`set/difference` over sets of logic variables (symbols) AND over sets of
result tuples; `kotobase.datom-plan` does `(reduce set/union #{} quad-sets)`
over sets of quad maps. Monomorphising needs one copy per element type, and
`datalog.core` passes `set/union` as a VALUE to `merge-with`, where there is
no call site to monomorphise at.

A second blocker is recorded separately and marked intentional:
`max-typed-set-items` is 32 and `:set-literal :language-semantics` is
`:bounded-unique-typed-set` -- the declared design, not a gap. A semi-naive
datalog fixpoint's candidate sets are result sets. Kept apart so a reader does
not expect these modules to migrate the day element-type polymorphism lands.

Recorded in `kotoba-lang/lang/surface-status.edn` `:other-gaps
:element-type-polymorphism` and `lang/q9-migration.edn` `:blocked-components`.

## Three things that looked like blockers and were spellings

Each was measured, and each would have been reported as a language gap by
anyone reading the refusal:

- **variadic**: `&` is refused only INSIDE a multi-arity declaration. A single
  variadic clause is admitted, so `union`/`difference`/`intersection` could
  keep their whole arity surface in one clause.
- **`every?`**: not an admitted head, but a typed set is walkable with
  `typed-set-nth` and `typed-set-count`.
- **`(set ...)`**: not a builtin. `into` is.

None of them is why those modules are blocked. Recorded so the next reader
does not spend the measurement again -- and because a gap list padded with
spellings makes the real blocker harder to see.

## What the fix uncovered, one defect behind

Admitting the protocol docstring moved `datom.source` from
`:kotoba.error/protocol-declaration` to `:kotoba.error/record-declaration`.
The new refusal is `(defrecord ^:no-doc SeqSource [quads] ...)` -- and the
inline protocol implementations in that same form are ADMITTED. Only the
name's metadata is not.

Measured: **the compiler's source reader does not implement metadata at all.**
`^` and the form after it are read as ordinary list elements, so every element
after the head shifts by two. Four heads, four misleading messages:

    (defn ^:private h [x :i64] :i64 x)
      \"function parameters must be a vector\"   form=h
    (def k ^:no-doc 1)
      \"constant must contain exactly one literal value\"
    (defrecord ^:no-doc Sq [side])
      \"defrecord requires a bounded name and field vector\"
    (defprotocol ^:no-doc IP (area [this]))
      \"defprotocol requires unique bounded (method [this ...]) signatures\"

Not one names metadata. A reader handed the first will look at `[x]`, which is
a vector; the name position holds the metadata and `h` has become the
parameters. Only the `form` in the ex-data shows the shift.

**Recorded, not fixed, and the reason matters.** This is NOT the same fix as
the docstring. A docstring carries no meaning a declaration depends on.
`^:private` does: `:public?` is decided by the head alone (`defn` public,
`defn-` private), so a reader that merely discarded metadata would turn every
`^:private` function into an EXPORTED one. Silently widening a module's public
surface is the failure Q9's whole-component rule exists to prevent. That owes
a reader change AND a decision about who honours `:private` -- a focused pass,
not a rider on a pin advance.

## The fifth repository

Advancing amu's kotoba-sema pin surfaced a stale assertion in amu:
`i64-bitwise-test/shift-count-admission-is-fail-closed` required a NON-LITERAL
shift count to be rejected, and kotoba-sema `03e7a6e` -- inside the advanced
range -- admits it.

That commit names four repositories. **amu is a fifth place the old rule was
asserted, and nothing reported it**, because a consumer's stale assertion only
fails when its pin moves. This is the same shape CLAUDE.md records about
foundation-library pins: a fix in main does not arrive until someone names the
new sha, and neither does a contradiction.

The test was updated rather than the admission reverted, because the language
decided it and fail-closed was preserved by MOVING where it is enforced: an
out-of-range literal is still a compile-time refusal, and a computed count
carries a backend range guard. The test's own note already said the old rule
was \"an admission choice, not a safety requirement\".

## Landed

- kotoba-sema **8b288047** -- protocol and method docstrings admitted, bounded.
  Suites: nbb 370/1413 and JVM 490/2280, both 0 failures. Five mutations;
  four caught by exactly the test that names the behaviour, and the fifth
  recorded as NOT a behaviour change rather than as a missing control.
- kotoba-lang **6579c98a** -- both gaps recorded with their measurements, and
  the three spellings recorded as not-blockers.
- amu **PR #930** -- pin 0470c524 -> 8b288047 and the fifth-repository test.
  Suite 1390/10448, 0 failures (10446 with 1 failure before the test update).
- west pins advanced: kotoba-sema, kotoba-lang.

## What did not move

**Admission on that corpus is still 0 of 29.** One class closed and became its
successor; nine files are blocked on a language gap that is now written down
instead of being rediscovered. The corpus did not get closer to compiling --
it got closer to being correctly described, which is the precondition for
deciding what to do about it.")))
